#What Should the Agent Actually See?

Fixing the action space solved one major problem.

The DQN was no longer overwhelmingly encouraged to make random character guesses before learning how to ask questions.

But after dealing with actions, I ran into another problem that was arguably even more important:

> What information should the agent receive about the current game?

This sounds like a simple implementation detail.

It was not.

The answer would determine what the agent was actually capable of learning.

If I gave the agent too little information, it would have no meaningful understanding of the current search space.

If I gave it too much, I risked giving it the exact information required to solve the problem directly.

Somewhere between those two extremes, I needed to find a useful state representation.

# The State Representation Problem

At every step of the game, the environment changes.

The agent asks a question:

> Is Region = Europe?

The answer might be:

> Yes.

Immediately, the candidate pool changes.

Before question: 887 candidates

After question: 158 candidates

The important thing is that the next decision depends on the new search space.

A question that was useful when 887 characters remained might be completely useless when only 158 remain.

For example, a feature may initially have a balanced distribution:

Feature A

Value 1: 50%
Value 2: 50%

After several eliminations, the remaining candidates might look like:

Feature A

Value 1: 95%
Value 2: 5%

The same question no longer has the same value.

So the RL agent needed some way of understanding the current structure of the candidate pool, not just the original dataset.

That was the core problem.

First Attempt: Keep the Agent Blind

My first instinct was to keep the observation as simple as possible.

I did not want to hand the agent the solution.

So the initial observation contained only high-level information such as:

Observation

├── asked_mask
└── remaining_ratio

The asked_mask told the agent which questions had already been used.

The remaining_ratio represented how much of the original candidate pool was still active.

For example:

Initial candidates: 4,200
Remaining candidates: 420

remaining_ratio = 0.10

My thinking was:

The agent knows what it has already asked and how far through the search process it is. Maybe it can learn a good sequence of questions from that.

This was an appealingly clean design.

The agent was not given:

character identities;
raw attributes;
the current candidate matrix;
manually calculated information gain.

It had to learn from a minimal representation of the environment.

But there was a major problem.

The Blind Agent Could Not See the Actual Decision Problem

The agent knew:

What questions have I asked?

How many candidates roughly remain?

But it did not know:

How are the remaining candidates distributed?

This distinction turned out to be critical.

Imagine two different episodes.

Both might have:

remaining_ratio = 0.25

Both might have the same set of previously asked questions.

From the agent's perspective, they could appear almost identical.

But internally, the remaining search spaces could be completely different.

Episode A
Question X would split:

50% / 50%
Episode B
The same Question X would split:

95% / 5%

Those are completely different decision situations.

Yet the blind agent had no way to distinguish them.

What It Actually Learned

Without information about the current structure of the search space, the agent could not really learn:

Given this state, which question should I ask?

Instead, it was pushed toward learning something closer to:

At this stage of the game, this question usually works well.

In other words, it could learn a static sequence of preferences.

Something like:

Ask Question A
        ↓
Ask Question B
        ↓
Ask Question C
        ↓
Ask Question D

That might work if every episode had the same underlying structure.

But the project included randomized character data and changing candidate distributions.

A fixed sequence could not reliably adapt.

This was one of the most important observations I made during the project:

The agent was not failing because it could not choose an action. It was failing because I had not given it enough information to know why one action was better than another in the current state.

The observation was too abstract.

I had successfully hidden the answer from the agent.

Unfortunately, I had also hidden much of the problem.

The Opposite Extreme: Give the Agent Everything

Now the information required to evaluate every possible question is directly available.

The agent could effectively inspect the candidate matrix and determine:

How many candidates match Question 1?
How many match Question 2?
How many match Question 3?
...

This created what I started thinking of as the cheating dilemma.

The goal was not to hide information for the sake of making the environment difficult.

The agent obviously needs information to make intelligent decisions.

But I did not want to build an experiment where the observation already contained the exact structure required to calculate the optimal heuristic.

That would blur the distinction between:

Learning a decision-making policy

and:

Being directly given enough raw data to reconstruct the solution

Two Opposite Failures

Neither extreme felt right.

So I needed to think about what information actually mattered.

What Does the Agent Really Need?

I went back to the heuristics.

The strongest heuristic strategies were not interested in the features of the character.

What mattered was the partition created by an action.

For every possible question, the important information was:

What fraction of the current candidate pool would match this question?

That was enough to describe how effectively a question could divide the search space.

The agent did not necessarily need to know specifics on these candidates.

It needed to understand what would happen to the search space if it selected each action.

That led to the representation I eventually settled on.

The Dynamic Split-Ratio Vector

For every available question, the environment calculates the fraction of active candidates that would answer Yes.

These values are collected into a vector:

split_ratios =
[
    0.50,
    0.12,
    0.48,
    0.91,
    0.33,
    ...
]

Each position corresponds to a possible question.

For example:

Action 0 → Is Sex = female?
            split ratio = 0.48

Action 1 → Is Region = Europe?
            split ratio = 0.50

Action 2 → Is Hair color = ginger?
            split ratio = 0.12

Action 3 → Is Industry = Film?
            split ratio = 0.91

The key idea was that these ratios are dynamic.

After every question, the active search space changes.

So the split ratios are recalculated.

The same question can have a completely different split ratio later in the game.

This allowed the agent to see the changing structure of the search problem.

Why This Representation Was Different

The split-ratio vector sits somewhere between the two earlier extremes.

It does not expose:

character names;
raw rows;
candidate identities;
individual feature combinations.

But it does expose something decision-relevant:

The current partitioning behaviour of every possible question.

That distinction was important to me.

The environment is not saying:

Here are all the characters. Calculate the best split yourself.

It is providing an abstract representation of the current decision landscape:

If you take Action 0 → approximately 50% match.

If you take Action 1 → approximately 12% match.

If you take Action 2 → approximately 48% match.

...

The RL agent still has to learn what those numbers mean.

It must learn from reward and experience that some patterns of split ratios are generally more useful than others.

The Connection to the Heuristic Baselines

This was also where the earlier heuristic experiments became useful in an unexpected way.

The information-based agents had already shown me that balanced partitions were important.

A split ratio close to:

0.50

creates an approximately even binary partition.

The split-ratio representation allowed the RL agent to observe the same structure of the decision problem that the heuristic agents were exploiting.

But there was an important difference.

The heuristic was explicitly programmed to calculate:

Which action gives the best information gain?

The RL agent was not directly given that rule.

It received the state representation and reward signals, then had to learn a policy relating those observations to actions.

That made the experiment more interesting to me.

The question was no longer simply:

Can I implement information gain?

I had already done that.

The question became:

Can an RL agent learn useful information-gathering behaviour from an abstract representation of the current search space?

Was This Still "Pure" RL?

This was something I thought about while designing the environment.

The split ratios are calculated by the environment.

They are not discovered from raw observations by the DQN.

So there is clearly some domain knowledge built into the state representation.

I do not see that as invalidating the RL experiment.

The environment designer always decides what constitutes an observation.

A chess agent is given a representation of the board.

A robot may be given joint positions and sensor readings.

Here, I chose to represent the deduction game through the current decision consequences of available questions.

The important distinction is that the environment does not tell the agent:

Action 7 is optimal.

Nor does it provide:

Information gain = 0.999

for every action.

Instead, it provides a representation of the current state from which a policy can learn which actions tend to produce better long-term outcomes.

Still, this is an important limitation of the experiment to acknowledge.

The representation is not neutral.

It encodes my understanding that the way an action partitions the search space is relevant to the problem.

That is something I want to be explicit about rather than hide.

A New Way of Thinking About State

This experiment changed how I thought about RL observations.

I initially treated observation design as something closer to an implementation choice:

What variables should I put in the observation dictionary?

By this point, I was starting to see it differently:

An observation is a hypothesis about what information matters for solving the task.

The blind observation made one hypothesis:

The agent only needs to know what it has already asked and how much of the search space remains.

That hypothesis was too weak.

The raw candidate matrix made another:

The agent should have access to the complete underlying state.

That risked making the representation unnecessarily explicit.

The split-ratio vector represented a third hypothesis:

The agent does not need candidate identities. It needs to understand how each possible action would currently partition the search space.

That was the representation I decided to experiment with.

The Next Question

By the end of this stage, I had a much better observation design.

The agent could now see a dynamic representation of the current search space.

The action space had been constrained so that exploration focused on meaningful questioning.

The remaining problem was:

How should I reward the behaviour I actually want?

Initially, it was tempting to reward the agent simply for eliminating candidates.

But that raised another question.

Is eliminating 100 candidates always equally valuable?

Consider:

4,200 → 4,100

versus:

101 → 1

Both eliminate 100 candidates.

But they clearly do not represent the same amount of progress.

The next stage of the project moved from state representation to reward representation.

I wanted the reward to reflect not just how many candidates disappeared, but how much uncertainty the agent had actually removed.
